From a0ee7825fa2ce0fe8633f4006c6f9e5290ada61d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 07:07:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] stage2: keep a progress crumb that survives a watchdog reset

A Stage 2 that hangs is reset by its watchdog, and its console log is a
RAM page the reset loses, so the next boot cannot tell where it stopped.
Record the step reached (entry, payload search, NVMe, kboot, PCIe and
its result, no payload, jump) in eight free bytes of the PMU's legacy
scratch (+0x28..+0x2f, after the kernel's own cells). At entry, a
record that never reached the jump is copied to the second half, so the
boot after a stall still shows where that one stopped. Only the ESP
Stage 2 writes it; Stage 1 has no room and gets empty inlines. Every
failure is ignored.

Assisted-by: LLM
